halui.program.ui-disable takes program flow away from the UIs, and only from
them.

A machine that hands program flow to a PLC or a panel still runs AXIS for
everything else, and a greyed-out button is not enough: keyboard shortcuts, a
second client and a stale view all reach the same REST calls.  So emccmd
refuses run, step, pause, resume, the abort of a running or paused program and
the optional-stop and block-delete toggles while the pin is high, unless the
caller passes force.

What must keep working is checked as carefully as what is refused: halui's
program pins, forced emccmd calls, the running program itself (the pin is not
a feed hold), and an abort that stops an MDI move -- that is not program flow,
and taking it away would leave the operator unable to stop a touch-off.

The pin is linked from the config's own HAL file to prove it exists by the
time that file runs.
